{"subjectId":"sql","id":"insert","title":"INSERT","group":"Defining & Changing Data","order":8,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>INSERT</code> adds rows — one at a time, many at once, or straight from a query.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-plus\"></i>Basic forms</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- always name the columns: robust to column order and future new columns\nINSERT INTO customers (customer_id, name, city, signup_date)\nVALUES (6, 'Farhan', 'Pune', '2024-04-02');\n\n-- several rows in one statement (one round trip, one transaction)\nINSERT INTO customers (customer_id, name, city, signup_date) VALUES\n    (7, 'Divya', 'Chennai', '2024-04-05'),\n    (8, 'Gautam', NULL,     '2024-04-07');\n\nSELECT * FROM customers WHERE customer_id &gt;= 6 ORDER BY customer_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>customer_id | name   | city    | signup_date\n------------+--------+---------+------------\n6           | Farhan | Pune    | 2024-04-02\n7           | Divya  | Chennai | 2024-04-05\n8           | Gautam | NULL    | 2024-04-07</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-key\"></i>Auto-generated keys and DEFAULTs</h3>\n<p>Omit the key column and the database generates it. In SQLite an <code>INTEGER PRIMARY KEY</code> auto-assigns <em>max + 1</em>; <code>RETURNING</code> hands back the generated values without a second query.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>INSERT INTO products (name, category, price)\nVALUES ('Keyboard', 'Electronics', 1800)\nRETURNING product_id, name;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>product_id | name\n-----------+---------\n8          | Keyboard</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>Get the new id</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PostgreSQL, SQLite 3.35+</td><td><code>INSERT … RETURNING id</code></td></tr><tr><td>SQL Server</td><td><code>INSERT … OUTPUT inserted.id VALUES …</code> or <code>SCOPE_IDENTITY()</code></td></tr><tr><td>MySQL</td><td><code>SELECT LAST_INSERT_ID()</code> after the insert</td></tr><tr><td>Oracle</td><td><code>RETURNING id INTO :v</code> (PL/SQL)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i>INSERT … SELECT</h3>\n<p>Copy rows produced by any query — no <code>VALUES</code> keyword.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE vip_customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, total_spent INTEGER);\n\nINSERT INTO vip_customers (customer_id, name, total_spent)\nSELECT c.customer_id, c.name, SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price)\nFROM customers c\nJOIN orders o       ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id\nJOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id   = o.order_id\nWHERE o.status &lt;&gt; 'cancelled'\nGROUP BY c.customer_id, c.name\nHAVING SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price) &gt; 50000;\n\nSELECT * FROM vip_customers ORDER BY total_spent DESC;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>customer_id | name   | total_spent\n------------+--------+------------\n1           | Ananya | 68400\n2           | Rahul  | 61200</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-filter-off\"></i>Skipping rows that would violate a constraint</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQLite</figcaption><pre><code>INSERT OR IGNORE INTO departments (dept_id, name, location)\nVALUES (1, 'Engineering', 'Bengaluru'), (5, 'Finance', 'Delhi');\n\nSELECT * FROM departments ORDER BY dept_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>dept_id | name        | location\n--------+-------------+----------\n1       | Engineering | Bengaluru\n2       | Sales       | Mumbai\n3       | HR          | Hyderabad\n4       | Marketing   | Pune\n5       | Finance     | Delhi</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>MySQL / PostgreSQL</figcaption><pre><code>INSERT IGNORE INTO departments (dept_id, name, location) VALUES (5, 'Finance', 'Delhi');          -- MySQL\nINSERT INTO departments (dept_id, name, location) VALUES (5, 'Finance', 'Delhi')\nON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;                                                                     -- PostgreSQL</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>INSERT INTO t VALUES (…)</code> without a column list depends on the physical column order — it breaks (or silently puts values in the wrong columns) after an <code>ALTER TABLE</code>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Batch inserts: one multi-row <code>INSERT</code> (or many inserts inside one transaction) is often 10–100× faster than autocommitting each row separately.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you insert data from one table into another?</summary><div><p><code>INSERT INTO target (cols…) SELECT cols… FROM source WHERE …;</code> — the column counts and types must line up. To create the target at the same time use <code>CREATE TABLE t AS SELECT …</code> (or <code>SELECT … INTO t</code> in SQL Server).</p></div></details></section>"}